  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset titled \"Isotropic 3D electron microscopy data of isolated murine pancreatic islets treated with low glucose\" has gained considerable attention within the academic community, amassing 12 citations since its publication in 2022. The work reflects high-visibility uptake, with notable dissemination in prestigious venues such as Nature Communications and bioRxiv. Additionally, the dataset has demonstrated substantial downstream reuse, being referenced in multiple articles and preprints.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2022, this dataset provides critical insights into the ultrastructure of pancreatic islets, specifically examining their response to low glucose conditions through advanced isotropic 3D electron microscopy techniques. The work was conducted by a collaborative group affiliated with the Howard Hughes Medical Institute, including prominent researchers such as Andreas Mueller and Michele Solimena.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset's impact is further underscored by its citations, which total 12 to date. This figure signals its relevance and utility in ongoing research, particularly in fields like endocrinology and cell biology. The data is not only foundational for understanding beta cell function and regulation but has also facilitated further studies, as evidenced by the reuse by 14 different scholarly works, showcasing its significance in advancing scientific knowledge in related areas.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Moreover, the dataset was shared early in its lifecycle, resulting in 10 citations from preprint publications. Despite limited engagement evidenced by only four views and no downloads, the ongoing interest and recognition highlight the value of this resource in the scientific community. The dataset is openly accessible on platforms such as Figshare, promoting knowledge sharing and collaboration in research.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">In conclusion, the isotropic 3D electron microscopy dataset serves as a vital resource for understanding the intricate biology of pancreatic islets under metabolic stress, while also illustrating the importance of open science practices in enhancing the reach and application of scholarly works. The dataset's citations from multiple high-profile venues indicate a strong trajectory of impact within its field, promoting further exploration of pancreatic health and disease.</p>",
